  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enabling democratic development by enhancing the effectiveness of public forum</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The government of Cambodia has adapted an ambitious set of policies and plans to develop the country along lines that are intended to benefit all citizens. The &#x201c;Rectangular Strategy&#x201d; and the National Strategic Development Plan set out a number of programmes with a primary goal to &#x201c;reduce poverty rapidly&#x201d; through good governance. At the core of the overall strategy is the concept of Decentralization and Deconcentration (D&amp;D), to improve efficiencies and good governance by shifting responsibilities from national ministries to local and sub-national decision makers.  The D&amp;D reforms present many challenges as well as historic opportunities for civil society to organize and demand rights under the laws, policies, and constitution of Cambodia.  The reforms give new duties, powers, and abilities to commune councils, and create an entirely new level of government, closer to the local level and with the potential to increase regional development and coordination.  Commune Council Support Project (CCSP) was established in 2000 with the aim of ensuring that decentralization and local governance reforms in Cambodia address local concerns, encourage local participation, and take account of local needs &#x2013; particularly those of the poorest members of the community &#x2013; and to ensure that civil society organisations are informed and responsive to this rapidly evolving social and political context.  CAFOD is supporting CCSP to disseminate information, promote  discussion and debate, provide training for civil society organisations working on D&amp;D, facilitate interaction and liaison between civil society and the government at all levels (from citizen reports to the commune council to feedback to the Ministry of Interior through the NGO Liaison Office), and to reward and recognize good governance within the civil society and local government sectors through workshops, publications, technical assistance, and a National Awards Programme.</narrative>
  </description>
